WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.880
Files are one of the most common ways applications exchange information.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:16.260
They arrive as uploads, attachments, imports, exports, logs, backups, evidence packages, reports, integration artifacts, images, documents, archives, and temporary working data.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.960
Because files feel ordinary, teams sometimes treat them as simple blobs.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:29.260
In practice, a file can carry data, metadata, paths, formats, sizes, and parser behavior that all affect security.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:35.560
File handling creates risk even in well-designed applications because it crosses many security boundaries at once.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.540
A user may control the filename.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.920
A browser may claim a content type.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.740
A third-party feed may send a format that is older than expected.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.440
An archive may expand into many files.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.500
A generated report may include more data than the recipient should receive.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:57.680
Each of those details can affect confidentiality, integrity, availability, privacy, and operations.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.100
User-controlled file content is only part of the concern.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.000
Metadata can be misleading.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.940
File names can contain unsafe characters.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.040
Paths can point outside intended storage.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.340
Sizes can exhaust resources.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.920
Formats can trigger parser bugs or unexpected behavior.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.060
Temporary files can linger.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.560
Logs can expose sensitive document names or content.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.500
Secure file handling means treating every part of the file workflow as security-relevant.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:37.400
A mature file-handling design combines validation, safe naming, controlled storage, least-privilege permissions, authorization checks, careful processing, monitoring, retention, and cleanup.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.420
The goal is not to block useful file workflows.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:50.640
The goal is to keep files from becoming an unreviewed path to data exposure, unauthorized access, unsafe execution, resource exhaustion, or operational failure.
